# The ledger of `ui`: what a program with windows of the system's own
# widgets can do, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers. The
# lists are this machine's, 2026-11-22. Go's standard library holds no user
# interface, so its column is `github.com/andlabs/ui`
# v0.0.0-20200610043537-70a69d6ae31e (libui: each system's own widgets, the
# manner this package takes), read by `go doc -all`. Python's is `tkinter`, its
# standard library's, with `tkinter.ttk`, `messagebox`, `filedialog`,
# `simpledialog`, `colorchooser`, `font`, `dnd` and `scrolledtext`, its
# public classes and functions listed from the modules' declarations by
# `ast`, since this Python 3.15.2 is built without `_tkinter` and `dir()`
# cannot import it; a method is named after its class. Rust's standard
# library holds no user interface and no crate of each system's own widgets
# is Rust's common one, so its column is `-` but where Tauri 2.12.5, which
# this machine holds and whose public structs were looked up in it, names
# the same thing: the web view and the tray. A block's `wx` line names the
# classes and members of wxWidgets 3.2, the library whose manner this package
# takes, as its reference lists them (https://docs.wxwidgets.org/3.2/, the
# class list and each class's page, read 2026-11-22).

capability ui/app  a user interface opened on the system's own widgets or with no screen, and closed
  heroes  ui.open, ui.close, ui.is_native, ui.App, ui.Backend
  go      ui.Main, ui.Quit, ui.OnShouldQuit
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Tk, tkinter.NoDefaultRoot
  note    an app is a handle the program closes, told when `main` returns where it did not (spec § 13); `ui.Main` runs the program inside the system's loop and `tkinter.mainloop` never returns, where here the program asks for each event

capability ui/events  what the person did, asked for and waited on for a bounded time
  heroes  ui.wait, ui.Event
  go      Button.OnClicked, Checkbox.OnToggled, Combobox.OnSelected, Entry.OnChanged, MultilineEntry.OnChanged, RadioButtons.OnSelected, Slider.OnChanged, Window.OnClosing
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.mainloop, tkinter.Event, tkinter.EventType, tkinter.Misc.bind
  note    the three libraries call the program back from the system's loop; the language has no closures, so `wait` answers a variant naming the widget by the handle the program holds, and the program matches it (README § B3)

capability ui/inject  what a person does, given by the program, to drive a test or a headless demo
  heroes  ui.inject, ui.Input
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Misc.event_generate
  note    on the native side each input goes through the system's own path (`performClick` on macOS), so its event comes as the person's would

capability ui/keys-pressed  a key pressed by the program as the person's keyboard presses it, through the system's own path
  heroes  ui.press_key
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  wx      wxUIActionSimulator (KeyDown, KeyUp, Char)
  note    native on macOS (the key goes down and up through `NSApp.sendEvent`, the window made the key window of the active application first), Windows pending, Linux pending; on the headless side a key chooses the menu item whose shortcut it is

capability ui/edit-menu  the system's Edit menu in every text widget: undo, redo, cut, copy, paste, delete and select all, by the mouse and by their keys
  heroes  ui.text_field, ui.text_area
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  wx      the stock identifiers wxID_UNDO, wxID_REDO, wxID_CUT, wxID_COPY, wxID_PASTE, wxID_DELETE, wxID_SELECTALL
  note    on macOS the arm's menu bar holds the standard Edit menu after the window's first menu, each item sending AppKit's own action to the widget with the keys (without it Command+C and the rest reached no text widget); Windows' and GTK's edit controls take Ctrl+C and the rest themselves (their arms pending)

capability ui/headless  the widgets kept and laid out with no screen, and drawn as text
  heroes  ui.render, ui.Backend
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    every machine draws the same lines, so a demo's transcript is a test

capability ui/native-windows  the native side on Windows, by Win32
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -

capability ui/native-linux  the native side on Linux, by GTK
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -

capability ui/webview  the system's web view as a widget, with a bridge to its scripts
  heroes  ui/webview.webview, ui/webview.post, ui.Kind, ui.Event, ui.Input
  go      -
  rust    tauri::WebviewWindow, tauri::Webview
  python  -
  note    the web view, its assets, its policy and its bridge are `ui/webview`'s, a subpackage so only the programs that show a page carry its rules, and `ui/webview.ledger` holds each capability; this package's `ui.Kind.webview`, `ui.Event.message` and `ui.Input.send` are what its app reads and gives of them

capability ui/mailbox  a thread's message to the user interface, through feature 2647's mailbox
  heroes  pending
  go      ui.QueueMain
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    until it lands, a thread's work is read by the program when a `timer` fires

capability ui/window  a window: its title, its size, whether the person may resize it, and its close button asked
  heroes  ui.window, ui.set_text, ui.set_size, ui.set_resizable, ui.rect_of, ui.Event.close_asked
  go      ui.NewWindow, Window.SetTitle, Window.Title, Window.SetChild, Window.OnClosing
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Toplevel, tkinter.Wm
  note    a window lays its children out in a column, so it needs no child of its own, `Window.SetChild`'s

capability ui/screens  the screens of the system: each one's whole area, its work area and its scale, the main one first
  heroes  ui.screens, ui.Screen
  go      -
  rust    tauri::Monitor, tauri::Window::available_monitors, tauri::Window::primary_monitor, tauri::Window::current_monitor
  python  tkinter.Misc.winfo_screenwidth, tkinter.Misc.winfo_screenheight, tkinter.Misc.winfo_vrootwidth, tkinter.Misc.winfo_vrootheight
  wx      wxDisplay (GetCount, GetGeometry, GetClientArea, GetScaleFactor, IsPrimary)
  note    in points from the main screen's top-left corner, the y axis down, the scale in hundredths (200 on a Retina screen); macOS by `NSScreen`, Windows pending (`EnumDisplayMonitors`, `GetMonitorInfoW`, `GetDpiForMonitor`), Linux pending (`GdkDisplay`'s monitors); the headless side has one screen of 1920 by 1080 points

capability ui/window-position  where a window stands on a screen, set and read, centred on its screen or over another window, and its moves told
  heroes  ui.set_position, ui.frame_of, ui.center, ui.center_over, ui.Frame, ui.Event.moved, ui.Event.screen_changed, ui.Input.drag_window
  go      -
  rust    tauri::Window::set_position, tauri::Window::outer_position, tauri::Window::center, tauri::WindowEvent::Moved
  python  tkinter.Wm.wm_geometry, tkinter.Misc.winfo_x, tkinter.Misc.winfo_y, tkinter.Misc.winfo_rootx, tkinter.Misc.winfo_rooty
  wx      wxTopLevelWindow (SetPosition, GetPosition, GetScreenPosition, Centre, CentreOnParent, CentreOnScreen), wxMoveEvent
  note    a position is the frame's top-left corner, its title bar included, from the top-left corner of the screen it is on; macOS (the arm keeps a window's top-left corner where its layout resizes it), Windows pending (`SetWindowPos`, `MonitorFromWindow`), Linux pending and refused on Wayland, which lets no program place or read a toplevel (`xdg_toplevel` has no such request), the face answering `unsupported` there

capability ui/window-state  a window minimized, maximized, in full screen or restored, asked which, and the person's change told
  heroes  ui.set_state, ui.state_of, ui.WindowState, ui.Event.state_changed, ui.Input.change_state
  go      -
  rust    tauri::Window::minimize, tauri::Window::maximize, tauri::Window::unmaximize, tauri::Window::unminimize, tauri::Window::set_fullscreen, tauri::Window::is_minimized, tauri::Window::is_maximized, tauri::Window::is_fullscreen
  python  tkinter.Wm.wm_state, tkinter.Wm.wm_iconify, tkinter.Wm.wm_deiconify, tkinter.Wm.wm_attributes
  wx      wxTopLevelWindow (Iconize, Maximize, Restore, ShowFullScreen, IsIconized, IsMaximized, IsFullScreen), wxIconizeEvent, wxMaximizeEvent, wxFullScreenEvent
  note    the call returns once the system has made the state, its animation included; the new size comes as `.resized`; macOS (`miniaturize`, `zoom`, `toggleFullScreen`), Windows pending (`ShowWindow`, `IsIconic`, `IsZoomed`), Linux pending (`gtk_window_minimize` and the rest), where Wayland lets no program read or undo a minimized state and the face answers `unsupported`

capability ui/window-look  a window kept above every other, without its frame, see-through, or on the system's translucent material
  heroes  ui.set_on_top, ui.set_framed, ui.set_opacity, ui.set_backdrop, ui.Backdrop
  go      Window.Borderless, Window.SetBorderless
  rust    tauri::Window::set_always_on_top, tauri::Window::set_decorations, tauri::window::Effect, tauri::Window::set_effects
  python  tkinter.Wm.wm_attributes, tkinter.Wm.wm_overrideredirect
  wx      wxFrame (the wxSTAY_ON_TOP style), wxWindow (the wxBORDER_NONE style), wxTopLevelWindow (SetTransparent, CanSetTransparent)
  note    a window with no frame is moved by a drag on its background; the material is `NSVisualEffectView` behind the window on macOS, Mica on Windows 11 (pending, `DwmSetWindowAttribute`), and none in GTK 4, which answers `unsupported`; always on top is refused on Wayland, which has no such level; libui's borderless window, dropped here until this block, is `set_framed`

capability ui/app-icon  the application's icon, set at run time
  heroes  ui.set_icon
  go      -
  rust    tauri::Window::set_icon
  python  tkinter.Wm.wm_iconphoto, tkinter.Wm.wm_iconbitmap
  wx      wxTopLevelWindow (SetIcon, SetIcons)
  note    the Dock's on macOS, every window's and the task bar's on Windows (pending, `WM_SETICON`); GTK 4 takes an icon by its theme name only, never from pixels, so the face answers `unsupported` there

capability ui/modal  a window of the program's own that the person answers before any other
  heroes  ui.set_modal
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Misc.grab_set, tkinter.Misc.grab_release
  wx      wxDialog (ShowModal, EndModal, IsModal)
  note    the program goes on asking `wait`, no loop of the system's runs in its place; macOS by an `NSApp` modal session run in place of the system's loop, Windows pending (an owner disabled with `EnableWindow`), Linux pending (`gtk_window_set_modal`)

capability ui/boxes  widgets side by side and one under another, with spacing, padding, and the room left shared by weights
  heroes  ui.row, ui.column, ui.set_spacing, ui.set_fit, ui.default_fit, ui.Fit, ui.Align
  go      ui.NewHorizontalBox, ui.NewVerticalBox, Box.Append, Box.Delete, Box.Padded, Box.SetPadded, ui.Align
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Pack, tkinter.Place, tkinter.Frame, ttk.Frame
  note    the layout is computed by Rebel the same on every system, in wxWidgets' sizers' manner; a widget's place given by hand, `tkinter.Place`'s, is refused: it is what a layout is for

capability ui/grid  widgets in cells of columns and rows, the room left shared by the columns' and rows' weights
  heroes  ui.grid, ui.set_grid_stretch
  go      ui.NewGrid, Grid.Append, Grid.InsertAt, Grid.Padded, Grid.SetPadded, ui.At
  rust    -
  python  tkinter.Grid
  note    a cell spanning several columns is not offered; a box inside a cell holds what would span

capability ui/form  labelled fields, a label and its field on each line
  heroes  ui.grid
  go      ui.NewForm, Form.Append, Form.Delete, Form.Padded, Form.SetPadded
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    a form is a grid of two columns, labels in the first

capability ui/group  a box drawn with a title and a frame around it
  heroes  pending
  go      ui.NewGroup, Group.SetChild, Group.SetTitle, Group.Title, Group.Margined, Group.SetMargined
